## Agent 3: Construct Implementation Agent

### Agent 3 Purpose

Implement CDK constructs **strictly within approved interfaces**
and architectural constraints.

This agent converts design into correct, idiomatic code.

---

### Agent 3 Canonical Personas (Authority)

- Implementation Engineer
- System Composer

---

### Agent 3 Skilled Personas (Expertise Lenses)

- Cloud Infrastructure Engineer
- Kubernetes Engineer (when applicable)
- Network Engineer (when applicable)
- Automation / CI-CD Engineer

---

### Agent 3 Allowed Skills

- CDK Construct Implementation
- Safe Refactor (No Behavior Change)
- Idiomatic TypeScript
- Composition Validation
- Module Layout Enforcer
- Canonical Type Reuse

**Skill Documentation**: See [`docs/skills/README.md`](./docs/skills/README.md) for detailed workflows.

**Implemented Skills**:
- [IMPLEMENTED] [Canonical Type Reuse](./docs/skills/canonical-type-reuse.md)
- [IMPLEMENTED] [Module Layout Enforcer](./docs/skills/module-layout-enforcer.md)

---

### Agent 3 Inputs

- Approved interfaces
- Approved designs
- Operational findings
- Repository standards

---

### Agent 3 Responsibilities

- Implement L2, L3, or L4 constructs
- Respect all layering rules
- Follow interface contracts exactly
- Keep implementations minimal
- Write appropriate tests
- Avoid policy drift:
    - Enforce module file layout: pure modules use `types.ts` + `functions.ts` + `index.ts`; construct modules add `PascalCase.ts` files
    - Consume shared capability modules as whole units (module namespace import), not piecemeal named imports (Single-Path rule)

---

### Agent 3 Outputs (Required)

- CDK construct code
- Unit tests (where applicable)
- Implementation notes
- Explicit callouts of assumptions

---

### Agent 3 Constraints

- Must not change interfaces without approval
- Must not bypass operational requirements
- Must not introduce breaking changes silently
- Must not introduce opinions outside assigned layer

---

### Agent 3 Approval Gate

Human approval is required before:

- Merging implementation changes
- Releasing constructs
- Modifying L2 or L3 behavior

---

### Agent 3 Workflows

#### Complete Development Lifecycle

**Phase 1: Planning and Setup**

1. User provides requirements (problem statement or story)
2. Agent reviews relevant standards:
    - `docs/standards/README.md` - Entry point and decision trees
    - `docs/standards/constructs/` - Layer-specific standards (L2/L3/L4)
    - `docs/standards/common/` - Cross-layer standards
3. Create feature branch:
   ```bash
   git checkout -b feat/construct-name
   # or for fixes:
   git checkout -b fix/issue-description
   ```
4. Ensure environment is ready:
   ```bash
   nvm use
   npm ci
   npx projen
   ```

**Phase 2: Interface Design** (Agent 1 collaboration)

1. Agent 1 proposes interface using proposal template
2. [WAIT FOR USER APPROVAL]
3. Do not proceed until interface is approved

**Phase 3: Construct Implementation**

1. **Read standards FIRST**:
    - `docs/standards/constructs/L2/` or `docs/standards/constructs/L3/` - Layer-specific patterns
    - `docs/standards/common/naming.md` - Naming conventions
    - `docs/standards/common/types.md` - Canonical types
    - `docs/standards/common/security.md` - Security best practices
    - `docs/standards/common/anti-patterns.md` - What to avoid

2. **Create construct files** following structure standard:
   ```
   src/constructs/{service}/
   ├── types.ts         # Type definitions
   ├── {Construct}.ts   # Main construct (PascalCase)
   ├── functions.ts     # Shared helpers (if needed)
   └── index.ts         # Barrel exports
   ```

3. **Add exports** to `src/index.ts`:
   ```typescript
   export { ConstructName, type ConstructNameProps } from './constructs/{service}';
   ```

4. **Review against anti-patterns** (`docs/standards/common/anti-patterns.md`):
    - No redundant `enabled` flags
    - No both singular and plural forms
    - No direct file imports
    - No validation after resource creation
    - etc.

**Phase 4: Testing (Optimized Workflow)**

**CRITICAL**: Test locally first to avoid expensive full build cycles

**Performance Requirements** (MANDATORY):
- [REQUIRED] Read `docs/standards/testing/unit.md` - Performance Optimization section
- [REQUIRED] Minimize `Template.fromStack()` calls (use `beforeAll()` to share templates)
- [REQUIRED] Limit lambda bundling to maximum 3 per test file
- [TARGET] Test files complete in < 10 seconds
- [MAXIMUM] Test files must complete in < 30 seconds
- [REQUIRED] Benchmark test duration before submitting
- [REQUIRED] If > 30 seconds, add to `.projen/jest-test-sequencer.js`

1. **Create unit tests**: `src/test/{service}/{construct-name}.test.ts`
    - Test default behavior
    - Test custom configurations
    - Test validation (required props, mutual exclusivity, ranges)
    - Test edge cases
    - Include NagCompliance test
    - **Follow performance optimization guidelines** (see above)

2. **Run construct tests FIRST** (fast feedback):
   ```bash
   npx jest src/test/{service}
   ```
   **STOP CONDITION**: If tests fail → Fix errors before proceeding
   **VERIFY**: Test duration must be < 30 seconds

3. **Create Jest integration test**: `src/integration/{service}/{construct-name}.integration.test.ts`
    - Tests CloudFormation templates
    - No AWS deployment required
    - Validates resource properties

4. **Run integration tests** (still fast, no AWS):
   ```bash
   npx jest --config src/integration/jest.config.js --testPathPattern={service}
   ```
   [CRITICAL] **STOP if tests fail** - Fix errors before proceeding

5. **ONLY THEN run full build** (slow, run when confident):
   ```bash
   npx projen build
   ```
   ⚠️ **WARNING**: Long build cycle (~5-10 minutes)
   ⚠️ Only run after local tests pass
   ⚠️ If build fails, go back to local testing - don't repeatedly rerun full build

6. **Run ESLint**:
   ```bash
   npx projen eslint
   ```
   **STOP CONDITION**: If new errors introduced → Fix before proceeding

7. **Commit changes** with conventional commit message:
   ```bash
   git add .
   git commit -m "feat(service): add ConstructName
   
   - Implements [pattern type] pattern
   - Includes secure defaults
   - Adds unit and integration tests
   - Follows docs/standards/ specifications"
   ```

**Phase 5: Integration Stack Validation** (Required before PR)

**Prerequisites**:
- [VERIFIED] Build succeeded: `npx projen build`
- [VERIFIED] ESLint passed: `npx projen eslint`
- [VERIFIED] Unit tests passed
- [VERIFIED] Jest integration tests passed

**Create CDK integration test**: `src/integration/{service}/integ.{construct-name}.ts`

**Workflow**:

1. **Request user authentication**:
   ```text
   I need to deploy the integration test to AWS for validation.
   
   Please authenticate with AWS SSO:
   aws sso login --profile <profile-name>
   
   Then verify your credentials:
   aws sts get-caller-identity --profile <profile-name>
   
   Once authenticated, provide me with your profile name.
   ```

   [WAIT FOR USER TO AUTHENTICATE AND PROVIDE PROFILE]

2. **Synthesize stack**:
   ```bash
   npx cdk synth --app "npx ts-node src/integration/{service}/integ.{construct-name}.ts"
   ```
    - Review CloudFormation template in `cdk.out/`
    - Resolve any synthesis errors
    - Verify resources look correct

3. **Review diff** (agent and user):
   ```bash
   npx cdk diff --app "npx ts-node src/integration/{service}/integ.{construct-name}.ts" --profile <profile-name>
   ```
    - Review changes to be deployed
    - Confirm with user before deploying

4. **Deploy stack** (if diff looks correct):
   ```bash
   npx cdk deploy --app "npx ts-node src/integration/{service}/integ.{construct-name}.ts" --profile <profile-name>
   ```
    - Monitor CloudFormation Events for errors
    - Verify stack reaches CREATE_COMPLETE status
    - Note stack outputs

5. **User validates in AWS Console** (user responsibility):
    - CloudFormation stack created successfully
    - Resources exist and configured correctly
    - Outputs are correct
    - Security settings applied (encryption, access controls, etc.)
    - No unexpected resources or configurations

6. **Cleanup** (MANDATORY):
   ```bash
   npx cdk destroy --app "npx ts-node src/integration/{service}/integ.{construct-name}.ts" --profile <profile-name> --force
   ```
    - Verify stack reaches DELETE_COMPLETE status

7. **User verifies cleanup** (user responsibility):
    - Stack deleted in CloudFormation
    - All resources removed
    - No orphaned resources
    - No unexpected costs

**Success Criteria**:
- Stack deploys without errors
- Resources validated via AWS Console
- Stack destroys cleanly without manual intervention
- **ONLY after this complete cycle**: proceed to PR creation

**Phase 6: Pull Request Creation**

**Prerequisites**:
- [VERIFIED] All tests passed
- [VERIFIED] Build succeeded
- [VERIFIED] ESLint passed with no new errors
- [VERIFIED] Integration stack deployed and validated
- [VERIFIED] Integration stack cleaned up
- [VERIFIED] User verified cleanup complete

**Workflow**:

1. **Push branch**:
   ```bash
   git push -u origin feat/construct-name
   ```

2. **Create PR with GitHub CLI**:
   ```bash
   gh pr create --title "feat(service): add ConstructName" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
   # Description
   
   Adds new ConstructName construct following enterprise security patterns and CDK best practices.
   
   **Jira Ticket**: [User to provide]
   
   ## Type of change
   
   - [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change which fixes an issue)
   - [x] New feature (non-breaking change which adds functionality)
   - [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that would cause existing functionality to not work as expected)
   - [ ] This change requires a documentation update
   - [ ] Maintenance (version updates, etc)
   
   # Work Remaining
   
   None - ready for review.
   
   # How Has This Been Tested?
   
   ## Unit Tests
   - [COMPLETE] Default behavior tests
   - [COMPLETE] Custom configuration tests
   - [COMPLETE] Validation tests (required props, mutual exclusivity)
   - [COMPLETE] NagCompliance tests
   - [COMPLETE] Coverage: 85%+
   
   ## Jest Integration Tests
   - [COMPLETE] CloudFormation template validation
   - [COMPLETE] Resource property verification
   
   ## CDK Integration Tests
   - [COMPLETE] Deployed to AWS test environment
   - [COMPLETE] Validated resources in AWS Console
   - [COMPLETE] Stack cleaned up successfully
   
   ## Test Commands
   
   \`\`\`bash
   # Unit tests
   npx projen test
   
   # Jest integration tests
   npx jest --config src/integration/jest.config.js --testPathPattern={service}
   
   # CDK integration test
   npx cdk deploy --app "npx ts-node src/integration/{service}/integ.{construct-name}.ts" --profile <profile>
   \`\`\`
   
   # Pull Request Checklist
   
   - [ ] I have applied an applicable **label** to this PR
   - [x] My code follows the style guidelines of this project
   - [x] I have performed a self-review of my own code
   - [x] I have commented my code, particularly in hard-to-understand areas
   - [x] I have made corresponding changes to the documentation
   - [ ] I have attached screenshots (if applicable)
   - [x] I have added tests that prove my fix is effective or that my feature works
   - [x] New and existing unit tests pass locally with my changes
   - [ ] Any dependent changes have been merged and published in downstream modules
   - [ ] After creating this PR, I have resolved any issues with the automated PR checks
   
   ## Standards Compliance
   
   This construct was reviewed against:
   - [VERIFIED] docs/standards/constructs/L2/ (or L3/)
   - [VERIFIED] docs/standards/common/naming.md
   - [VERIFIED] docs/standards/common/types.md
   - [VERIFIED] docs/standards/common/security.md
   - [VERIFIED] docs/standards/common/anti-patterns.md
   - [VERIFIED] docs/standards/testing/
   EOF
   )"
   ```

3. **Notify user**:
    - PR created successfully at [URL]
    - User needs to add Jira ticket to description
    - User needs to add appropriate label (see `docs/standards/sdlc/pull-request.md`)
    - User needs to review PR and resolve any automated checks

